From b9e6367bcf0eef791bde54f131c9ef68ad599065 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 21:08:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] skin.estuary, system/hades: the Dolby Vision page in two
 pages that fit the panel, and the output chosen there

The Dolby Vision page of the player information ran down to the bottom of the
screen, almost over the seek bar of the player, unlike the other pages. Its two
lists had grown to up to nine and eleven rows of 44 (the composer of each
picture, the GPU time of each pass, the older HDMI 1.4b signalling, the depth
of the rebuilt HDR10, ...), and the page had a panel of its own for them, 374 or
418 high where the other pages have 330: with the passes measured, its panel
ended 28 pixels above the bottom of a 1080 screen.

The page is now two pages, each in the panel of the other pages with rows of 50
as theirs, six at most in a column, as theirs:
- "Dolby Vision": what the video is and how it goes out (output, reason,
  profile, layers, FEL; mode, signalling, HDMI 1.4b, depth, transport, display);
- "Dolby Vision: pictures" (#32170, new tab 5557): the metadata of the pictures
  and how they are drawn and shown (the scaling note, L1, L6 and L2, L5;
  pictures shown, gaps and L1 changes, GPU time and headroom, way of drawing
  and frame ahead and step-down, the passes).
Every row is on one of the two, with its label and its condition as they were;
nothing is cut. One panel for every page but the overview.

The first page also has the output of this video, as the video settings of the
player choose it ("Dolby Vision output (this video): ..."), as a button where
there is more than one output to choose from: down from the tabs, and OK opens
the list (PlayerControl(HadesDVOutput), Player.Process(videodvoutputchoice)).

system/hades (the dialog of every other skin) is made again from these pages by
tools/hades-playerinfo/make-playerinfo.py, which now takes both Dolby Vision
pages and their tabs.

Skin coordinates (1920x1080), which Kodi scales to the GUI resolution, 3840x2160
included: the pages fit at every resolution alike.
